WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:05.900
A&amp;E's reality hit Storage Wars revolves around
the unknown, the bizarre, and the surprising,

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.120
all of which lay safely ensconced within abandoned
lockers.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.540
Despite the plethora of oddball items, there
are certain discoveries that were bound to

00:00:13.549 --> 00:00:16.150
rise to the surface as exceptionally noteworthy.

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:20.980
Barry Weiss, who is known as "The Collector,"
may not be the most successful character on

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:25.600
the show, but he certainly makes up for it
with his entertaining commentary, and hilariously

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.060
questionable bidding decisions.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:28.580
"This has become personal.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:35.340
This has become personal…$3,000."

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:40.320
All of this was on full display when Weiss
arrived late to an auction and quickly snapped

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.350
up what appeared to be a worthless locker
for an inflated $1,525.

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:49.489
But Weiss' seemingly harebrained mistake turned
out to be quite on purpose.

00:00:49.489 --> 00:00:56.980
"You can only see somebody's mouth that's
open with teeth, and that's all you can see.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:57.980
But I want it."

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:02.360
The Collector had spotted an interesting statue
in the back, and his suspicions were quickly

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:02.920
confirmed.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.840
"This piece, got to be a one of a kind."

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:10.240
The statue had real dentures and glass eyes,
and its head had been hollowed out to house

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:11.370
its own little scene.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:15.930
"There's pictures on the wall, there's a table
with a lamp, there's the moon out the window…"

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:16.930
"It's incredible!"

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:17.420
"... and there's this little troll."

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:22.520
The $6,000 appraisal that Weiss received wasn't
enough to tempt his offbeat tastes, and the

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:24.990
Collector chose to keep it for his own private
collection.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:28.280
"Buddy, I'll see ya later."

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:28.960
"Take care."

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:33.080
Elvis Presley was the King of Rock n' Roll,
and there's no doubt that anything authentically

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.660
relating to his life and death is going to
carry some inherent value.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:41.030
But it was Storage Wars' resident bad boy,
David Hester, that walked away with the Presley

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:42.480
motherload in Season One.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:47.780
"My curiosity is up because somebody has these
newspapers wrapped and banded.

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:50.299
So I'm thinkin it's some type of collectible."

00:01:50.299 --> 00:01:54.380
Hester managed to score a locker filled with
thousands of newspapers featuring Elvis on

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:55.380
the front page.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:57.979
But it was the publication date that really
mattered here.

00:01:57.979 --> 00:02:04.250
The vast collection of periodicals were all
dated August 16, 1977, the day Elvis died.

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:08.880
"This is something that is just gonna add
up to hundreds of dollars, or thousands of

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.310
dollars, or even tens of thousands of dollars
I think."

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:16.770
With such a large quantity of highly-valued
newspapers, Hester knew he had a fortune on

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:20.670
his hands, as proved to be the case when the
vault of rock and roll treasures was valued

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:22.880
at a whopping $90,000.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:28.320
While Storage Wars and its spin-offs bring
treasure seekers to a wide variety of locales,

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:30.599
they've yet to venture into the deserts of
Asia.

00:02:30.599 --> 00:02:35.380
Thus, one can imagine the surprise when the
ever-eccentric Mary Padian found a pair of

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:38.489
camel saddles in a locker during the show's
eighth season.

00:02:38.489 --> 00:02:39.690
"These are so cool.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:43.349
I just gotta figure out what the heck they
are, so I know where to take 'em."

00:02:43.349 --> 00:02:47.320
The antiques became even more interesting
when she took them to be evaluated by a camel

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:47.820
owner.

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:52.180
Apart from being worth a pretty penny, the
rare valuables turned out to be specifically

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.810
made for shaggy, two-humped Bactrian camels.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:59.400
This species of camel is native to the rocky
deserts of Central and East Asia, and are

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:02.260
among the only truly wild camels still in
existence.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:03.800
"3,000?

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:05.460
That's double.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:07.960
Double hump, I'm pumped."

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:12.280
The Storage Wars crew has seen a lot, but
they weren't prepared to encounter the occult.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:16.900
At least, that seems to be the case judging
from Brandi Passante's face after she bought

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:21.880
a $900 locker midway through Season 5 that
contained some voodoo paraphernalia.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.340
"I don't know what this spooky s--- is, but
I'm not touchin' it."

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:28.720
As Brandi and her team dug their way down
through the initial layers of worthless crap,

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.740
they were surprised to find a small skull
and antlers that immediately spooked them

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:33.240
out.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:35.760
"This feels like some bad juju voodoo."

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:40.440
The supernatural concerns continued to mount
as the voodoo collection was further unveiled.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:45.120
From trinkets and statues to chicken bones
and beaks, the witch doctor's stash was about

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.880
as unsettling as it gets.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:50.800
"Maybe this is what the chief holds when he
sacrifices a virgin?"

